I still rely mostly on a stroke of luck(or two) in order
to ascend a wizard, and I haven't bothered to perfect
my wizard strategies... yet.

Knights seem to provide a good difficulty level for me,
even without any major conducts. Well, at least for
the early game:

Pros:
+1 Long Sword, easily made into Excalibur
+1 Lance, good for poking floating eyes (among other things)
Two-weapon combat (wombating) and the occasional JOUSTING.
Spellcasting abilities!
Possibility to effectively use a steed (not used this time).

Cons:
In the beginning, knight is only slighly better fighter than a wimp.
Hit points may become depleted rapidly in combat.
Chivalry provides a minor problem (those small penalties).
But hey, they are mostly early game problems...

This game was played for the fun of it.
BLAT! : "This game DOTH provide more mirth than a cask
of jackanapes. Verily."

Zan the Knight was brought up in the time of King Arthur... well, you
know, in England. Alas, her best friend was killed by a lynx, and
Lugh failed to help. Also, this attempt at a story is not very convincing.

// The mumbo-jumbo generator almost got overheated.

During her knight training, Zan mostly cleaned up the stables. 
That left her dexterity, strenght and constitution low, but as
she talked to the horses there, her int and wis went up like quicksilver
in Sahara desert. 

During that time England suffered from famine.
Luckily, Zan could catch nearly-starved rats every now and then,
a game which Zan would play later in the Dungeons of Doom
later on.

And so the time for Zan's graduation at the Knight Academy.
Armed with a long sword which she could hardly swing and
accompanied by a pony named Bill(KIA), Zan took her first steps
in the Dungeons of Doom. The words of King Arthur's pages
rang in her ears: "Retrieve the Amulet of Yendor and sacrifice it
to Lugh. Good luck."

The Dungeons proved to be suspiciously lively surroundings.
Two ancient altars lay on the very first level of the strange dungeons.
They both were white; Zan knew that Lugh would approve the sacrifices
offered on these altars. 

The dungeons forked in the second level. One stairway led to an irregular-
looking mining area, inhabited by peaceful dwarves and hostile gnomes.
Levels 3,4 and 6 contained shops where many interesting things could
be bought. And behold! A mysterious Oracle resided on level 5. 
Zan could barely make sense of what she said.

A shop in the Minetown provided Zan with a bag which made carrying
things very easy. Before venturing below Minetown, Zan poked
fountains with her long sword. Suddendly, a hand reaching from a fountain
grabbed the sword. "Hey! Give it back!.... Wait, this is not my sword.
Mine was rusty."

Level 9 contained an armour shop, and Zan bought a cloak of protection,
and placed her elven cloak in her BoH. 

A nymph fell to the Excalibur and Zan ate the corpse. "I'll find a tengu
in Sokoban, and it's supposed to be a no-teleport zone, so no problem."
Teleportitis was an annoyance, because the tengu remained unfound
until the quest portal on level 12...

Now Zan conquered Sokoban. Tired and gushed by orc blood, Zan pushed
the boulders around. But alas - a monster was trapped behind a boulder
in a narrow passageway. Frustrated, she threw her daggers and lance
at the monster's location. Eventually the monster was killed, but the lance
was lost in a pit, under the boulder. From Sokoban Zan found an amulet
of reflection. Zan had to break some boulders, however, and she went
to dug the lance out from the ground, digging a total of two pits.

Miraculously, level 12, a big elliptical room contained the portal to 
Joe Camel Castle. Zan threw an ice box on the castle courtyard, to
be left there. As said before, a tengu appeared. Zan gobbled it up,
and felt in control of herself. Great ! Now conquering the Mines was
no problem.

On level 14, Zan took time to examine her possessions (not for
the last time). Wow, a cursed scroll of genocide... ok, let's get
close to the staircase and summon some gray dragons.
And about 5 gray dragons appeared around Zan. She started
tickling one of the dragons, but it seemed that the dragons
disliked this and fought back. Luckily Zan survived the initial
hits and ran the stairs down. 

Donning a ring of conflict on, Zan collected her HP and climbed
the stairs back up. She teleported in a narrow tunnel, careful not
to enter a friendly temple nearby, and let the dragons have it.
3 drakes dropped scales... and now you can guess whether she
produced herself a nice Gray Dragon Scale Mail. 

Exploring level 15 found an interesting altar. Wiping the dust off
from the altar, Zan discovered that it was stained with blood.
However, the blood splatterings were oddly shaped, almost symmetrical.
And the obsidian stone felt warm and pleasant to touch. 
It was dark, and Zan could feel the runes on the altar twitch and
warp. And she knew what was written there: simple, clear words
which defied the teachings of Law and all she had consumed
during her years in the Knight Academy. Withdraw your hand...!
For now...

Level 16: What is this ? A lamp! It looks like as if it was produced
somewhere in the Middle East. I wonder if a djinni is in ? Hellooo?
A djinni emerged from the lamp! It spoke : "Greetings. Have you
seen Daffy Duck ?" "No... who is...", replied Zan. "Never mind. I
will grant you a wish for releasing me." "I would like to have speed
boots." "Here you go."

A strange aura filled the air on level 20. Wearing a towel, Zan
sensed a priest of Chaos somewhere on the level, and decided
to ignore the priest for the time being.

>From level 22 Zan found a portal to a great castle. It was Fort Ludios.
Excellent! Great! Superb! Money, polypiling, stash, etc. etc...
Zan didn't need money at the time, so she ignored Ludios for now.

Level 25 was surprisingly filled with water, with a small island in the
middle. Carefully entering the small keep on the island, Zan wore
a towel around her head. A Knight is supposed to be familiar with
various folklore, after all... 
Hack! Slash! Medusa, the horrible creature of the island,
lay slain on the ground. Zan had removed her amulet, and took 
a trophy by carefully tinning the gorgon's corpse.

"Nothing will stop me now. But why, why do I feel a pang of
guilt if I finish off a fleeing foe ? This will not do! I am not
content with Lugh." Now, that was a strange monologue
to Mickey the giant rat. "Squeek", thought Mickey. "A 
two-legged gone mad. Mickey mad with hunger. Mickey
fear for hideout. A bite will do." Squeeking, Mickey attacked Zan.
Stomp! A boot fell on Mickey's neck, not quite killing him.
"What is this? Didn't I finish you off while cleaning the stables?
Why did you come back?" Zan was cackling by now. Excalibur
wept as Zan drove it's point through Mickey's neck and lifted
the corpse up. A few seconds later Zan wiped blood and fur
from her mouth, her stomach feeling oddly content.

Where are the ending stats? Conducts? Vanquished creatures?
Attributes ? All in good time. Zan decided to visit the Camelot
Castle. King Arthur, upon seeing Zan, greeted her with concern
in his voice. "Well met, Zan. As you probably know, the foul
beast, Ixoth, has attacked the Camelot Castle. As it is..."

The Magic Mirror of Merlin had been lost, several knights slain,
yadda yadda yadda. What chance would Zan have, if the
Camelot Castle guards had failed not only to kill Ixoth, but to save
the Mirror as well ? A strange voice whispered to Zan:
"Because they lack the vision! I am Manannan Mac Lir... or merely an
avatar of myself. Listen to me, Zan. I know about the Mirror, and would
like to discover it's secrets. Bring the Mirror to me, but before that, honor
our agreement, if you will, by offering one of Lugh's beasts to me."
Shuddering, Zan descended the stairs. She travelled past the Isle of Glass,
and arrived at the Ixoth's lair. Ixoth sensed Zan and teleported itself
next to her. "A human! Well, this is interesting. Will your inventions, your
artificial claws and fangs save you ? I think not." The great beast snapped
at Zan, but it was unaware that Zan had also mastered transportation without
feet. Instead of biting Zan's leg off, it swalloved an apple which was innocently
lying on the ground. "Come back, intruder!" Ixoth followed Zan. The little
human was blocking the only exit... how come she was that fast ? Ixoth knew
that the human was also heavy, and the skin! It was reinforced with dragon hide?!
What an insult to Ixoth's kin! And that she couldn't be made to escape!
It wasn't in this knight's programming... one of us will die in this fight!

Excalibur stung Ixoth. Then it started cutting, and finally it was inflicting
deep wounds, and Ixoth knew that they would never heal.
The foul drake, waving it's claws and jaws madly, managed to bite Zan on
the left arm. No! Even the blood tastes all wrong - where is the taste of fear?
Is this the end of Ixoth... Excalibur thrust once more, this time in Ixoth's
throat. The beast instinctively swalloved it's own blood and stifled a cry
of pain. Then there was no need to do that. The Mirror came loose
from Ixoth's mighty grip. 

Weary from the victory, Zan barely greeted King Arthur...
Zan dragged herself to level 15 and summoned some white unicorns
with a cursed scroll of genocide. She felt guilty about killing them,
but after he offered the corpses on the black altar, the guilt
was gone. Forever. Manannan Mac Lir had accepted her allegiance.
After several more sacrifices (both physical and mental), an unseen
entity spoke: "Well, well. It seems that you have at the very least 
managed to relieve me of my boredom, but I have a reason to
believe that you might do us both a favour by completing your
main quest. Bring me the Amulet of Yendor, which you intended
to offer to that pesky entity which I will not name this time.
A deal is a deal is a deal. I have made many good deals
these times myself. I hereby present you my latest acquisition.
Touch the hilt and you will see."
A black sword appeared at Zan's feet. She stood by the altar,
staring at the sword. The red runes shone an eerie, barely
distinguishable dark red light. 

Zan would not argue, she would pick the blade up and place
it safely in the bag of holding. 

Not quite!

As her fingers tightened around the hilt of the sword, another voice,
this time more mysterious and vague, seemed to whisper an 
ancient and foreign name. For Zan, the name (apart from being
ominous in itself) had had no meaning before this moment. The
name was spoken in a language from another time, another place. 
"Stormbringer. Who did name me?"

"I can't do this", thought Zan. "Now I will walk away from this place.
Just a couple of more steps. I already can make out the daylight."
But the terrible, unfamiliar voice manifested itself again:
*Before you leave, sweet Zan, you might want to have a second
opinion! Open that spot from the floor! Yes, that one! Marked by
that heavy stone! You have a meaning for it, yes, I know."
Zan approached the ancient grave on level 2, and with haste
dug the grave open. A well-preserved humanoid corpse lay 
in the bottom of the pit. Zan pointed a wand of undead turning
at the corpse and made a gesture with her free hand - her right
hand would keep Stormbringer "safe and sound."
The corpse sprang to life - but the word "life" felt blasphemous
in this place. 
*I knew that barbarian well. We share a past. I share a past with
many. Do you want to share a past with me ? Or perhaps a future ?
An eternity ? Keep the blade and do what you set out to do!
Or abandon it! Walk away! Do mortals fade away on this plane?
Yes, I thought so. Look at the corpse you have just revived.
Your future has more potential. Why spend the rest of your
physical existence in some nameless pit ? Use your sword-hand,
send that creature back into the grave it belongs, and you will
understand!" 
At this, the undead barbarian sprang at Zan, his hands flailing
madly. Without much passion, Zan swung her black blade and
caught the corpse between it's shoulders. Stormbringer was
not satisfied, but Zan understood. The blade and the air particles
produced sweet music, and the sound of the breaking flesh
was the sound of universes colliding into each other. The dusty
gasp of the corpse was the war of air demons. Stormbringer
was not satisfied, and it made no effort to hide it's thirst.

Again Zan took the stairs into the Gnomish Mines. The dwarves,
mining gold and gems, seemed to not notice Zan, at least not
during their lives. Zan quickly noticed that she didn't have to
make the decision of taking her new found foes' lives.
Would it be wise to keep Stormbringer sheathed ? At the very least
it would save the lives of peaceful beings...

In the Minetown, Zan entered the stores, one by one, and picked
up an item of almost no value. Then she teleported out of the shops.
An alarm sounded many times! "What devilry is this?" Out of nowhere,
a band of Kops appeared. They wielded ancient weapons called
Clubs and Rubber Hoses. They attempted to blind their pray by the
devil's cream pies. Still, they were no match for Zan. Stormbringer
cried and made dying Kops cry. A Kop Kaptain threw a pie at Zan,
but she severed his head and picked the remaining pies up. 
"So, you wish to fight with less challenge? Let me help you."
Zan (a)pplied a cream pie, hitting a pie on her face. She could
not tell if it was telepathy or Stormbringer which guided her
during the rest of the battle, but what was the point of even
speculating about such trivial matters ? 

Zan thought about her situation. Armor ? Superb, dragon hide,
power gloves, speed boots, magic cloak, helm of brilliance, not
bad at all. Weaponry ? Saber of silver, a runesword which mocked
light itself by it's blackness, only occasionally coloured by ruby red
markings. And runes, too. And lots of magical wands, amulets, rings,
scrolls, spellbooks and water blessed by Manannan Mac Lir himself. But
something was missing. "I will make use of some of the rules to which this
world has been subjected to. To Camelot Castle..."
In the Castle, Zan locked herself in a room and made sure that no
enemies were nearby. She summoned some strange creatures
which boosted her health radically. "Take your shirt off, please."

After leaving the Camelot Castle, Zan met a knight. Was it for
the first or the second time, she could not tell. What was the knight,
anyway ? A doppelganger? A demon in disguise? If not, why had the
knight left the Castle, why had he abandoned his post ? Before the knight
could talk about dungeon exploration, Zan cut him open with her
runesword, which drank the knight's soul. "So, at least it had a soul."

What of Fort Ludios? It proved to be a great place for polypiling
and alchemy, further boosting Zan's health. But the place was
infested with gremlins... they were constantly multiplying whenever
they came into contact with water. "What curious creatures. Think
of an army of those in a battle, Stormbringer! If only they were stronger.
But I can't allow those creatures to thrive here. I will make this place
my sanctum when I achieve... immortality!"
*No! Do not spend any more time here than you have to! If you must,
then kill those beings!*
But Zan ignored Stormbringer and wore a ring of conflict. The creatures
turned against each other, but for every dead gremlin, ten more would
spring to life from the nearby moat. So Zan started freezing the water
with magical wands. After they were depleted, Zan called the help
of earth elementals and ruined the moat with boulders. 
A purple worm, also affected by the devil's ring, ate the gremlins,
and Zan could hear a belch every now and then. After minutes or
hours the gremlins were no more. 

The soldiers and officers provided to be good sacrifices. Not every
one of them was stationed on Ludios, and a few wandering patrols
ended up on the now black altars around the Dungeons. The first
two sacrifices summoned Yeenoghu and Juiblex. Zan was terrified
at their appearance, then merely ashamed of her reactions despite
her experience. "This will not do! I fear you not, forgotten demons!
Defend yourselves!" Yeenoghu screamed, and not only because
of his annoyance. Juiblex would have screamed, if he could have
done that. Nevertheless, both of the lowly demons perished and were
not seen in the Dungeons of Doom, or the world outside them ever
again.

Further human sacrifices produced interesting results; two incubi
appeared near the Minetown altar, after the priest of Lugh disappeared
in flames. "This newsgroup is not the place to discuss of what
happened between these individuals", said George M. Shrubbery.
"The following lines will describe the event just fine:"

